<p>The Trump administration said it will step up enforcement against foreign companies it says are extracting capabilities from U.S. artificial intelligence systems. White House technology officials accused firms in China of running &quot;industrial-scale&quot; efforts to copy American models through distillation. The accusation came in a White House memorandum from Office of Science and Technology Policy Director Michael Kratsios, as Washington casts AI as both a strategic race and a national security issue. The move adds tension ahead of an expected summit between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping in Beijing in the coming weeks, according to the reports. U.S. officials say the crackdown is meant to protect domestic innovation and preserve America’s lead in advanced AI, even as reporting notes that China has been closing the gap with the United States in the sector.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Distillation focus: The White House memo centers on model distillation, a technique in which developers use outputs from a larger system to build a smaller model with similar capabilities. Officials say Chinese firms have used that process improperly.</li><li>Race context: NPR reported that the administration singled out China at a moment when Beijing is closing the performance gap with U.S. AI companies, giving the policy move broader strategic weight beyond a single enforcement action.</li><li>Memo timing: The accusation landed just weeks before President Trump is set to meet President Xi in Beijing, tying a technical dispute to a sensitive diplomatic calendar.</li><li>Enforcement pledge: Administration officials said Washington plans to crack down on foreign tech companies it believes are exploiting U.S. AI models, signaling tougher controls or penalties could be ahead.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House Office of Science and Technology Policy: The office said foreign entities, mainly in China, are wrongfully distilling U.S. AI models and described the issue as large-scale theft of American technology. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cpqxgxx9nrqo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Trump administration: Administration officials framed the planned crackdown as a way to stop foreign firms from exploiting U.S. models and defend American leadership in AI. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/24/g-s1-118582/administration-crackdown-on-chinese-firms-exploiting-u-s-ai-models'>NPR</a>)</li><li>Media analysis: Coverage highlighted that the accusation comes shortly before an expected Trump-Xi summit, suggesting the issue could shape the tone of upcoming U.S.-China talks. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-23-0700/technology-white-house-accuses-china-industrial-scale-ai-technology-theft-weeks-ahead-trump-xi-summit'>AllSides</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/china-stealing-us-ai-technology-accuses-white-house-official'>China stealing US AI technology, accuses White House official</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/24/g-s1-118582/administration-crackdown-on-chinese-firms-exploiting-u-s-ai-models'>Trump administration vows crackdown on Chinese firms &#x27;exploiting&#x27; U.S. AI models</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-23-0700/technology-white-house-accuses-china-industrial-scale-ai-technology-theft-weeks-ahead-trump-xi-summit'>White House accuses China of &#x27;industrial-scale&#x27; AI technology theft weeks ahead of Trump-Xi summit</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cpqxgxx9nrqo'>White House memo claims mass AI theft by Chinese firms</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/23/2026/us-accuses-china-of-ai-theft'>US accuses China of AI theft</a> - semafor.com</li></ul>